      "text": "To find out how many students did not receive an A in either math or history, let's break the problem down into smaller parts and solve it step-by-step.\n\n1. **Count the number of students who received an A in history**:\n   It is given that 7 students received an A in history. \n\n2. **Count the number of students who received an A in math**:\n   Similarly, it is given that 13 students received an A in math.\n\n3. **Count how many students received an A in both courses**:\n   It is given that 4 students received an A in both math and history events.\n\nNext, we need to find out how many students received an A in at least one of the courses. To do this, we will use the principle of inclusion and exclusion.\n\n4. **Calculate the number of students who received an A in at least one course** using the principle of inclusion and exclusion:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Number of students with at least one A} = \\left(\\text{Number of students with History A} \\right) + \\left(\\text{Number of students with Math A} \\right) - \\left(\\text{Number of students with A in both courses}\n   \\right)\n   \\]\n   Substituting the given values:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Number of students with at least one A} = 7 + 13 - 4 = 16\n   \\]\n\n5. **Subtract the number of students who received an A in at least one course from the total number of students to get the number of students who did not receive an A in any of the courses**:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Number of students with no A} = \\text{Total number of students} - \\text{Number of students with at least one A}\n   \\]\n   Thus,\n   \\[\n   \\text{Number of students with no A} = 30 - 16 = 14\n   \\]\n\nFinal Answer:\n\\[\n\\boxed{14}\n\\]",
